  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "$\\int \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{7{x}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x=$",
  "worked_solution": "This integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arsinh}\u2061u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $7{x}^{2}={(\\sqrt{7}\\,x)}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\sqrt{7}\\,x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt{7}\\,\u27f9\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{7{x}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{7}\\,x)}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}}\\,\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arsinh}\u2061u+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arsinh}\u2061(\\sqrt{7}\\,x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
